// Syllabus — Rick Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples, 5th edition (2019). Book-anchored: numbers are Durrett's.
//
// SOURCE. The author's free PDF, https://services.math.duke.edu/~rtd/PTE/PTE5_011119.pdf (490 pp., "Version 5", built
// 28 Mar 2019), linked from https://services.math.duke.edu/~rtd/PTE/pte.html. The PDF has nine chapters plus Appendix A;
// note that its Chapter 8 is titled "Applications to Random Walk" (the web page's TOC says "Brownian Embeddings and
// Applications") and that Section 9.6 (Green's functions and potential kernels) contains no numbered item.
//
// EXTRACTION. `pdftohtml -xml` gives every text run with its font; item labels are the runs in CMBX12 (bold, 18 px) that
// start a line and read "Theorem|Lemma|Corollary|Example|Remark c.s.n."; inline exercises ("Exercise 2.3.1.") and the
// end-of-section exercise lists ("2.3.1.") are also bold but use a separate counter and were discarded, as were displayed
// equations "(c.s.n)", which have their own counter too. Durrett numbers theorems, lemmas, corollaries and examples with
// ONE counter per section, so the numbers below have gaps (the gaps are examples that were left out). The text between
// consecutive labels was scanned for "Theorem/Lemma/Corollary/Example c.s.n" citations to seed `deps`; every list was
// then checked by hand against the proof (`pdftotext -layout` for reading). In the PDF: 451 numbered results
// (Theorem 365, Lemma 83, Corollary 3) and 194 numbered examples, plus 4 numbered remarks in Chapter 8.
//
// WHAT IS LISTED. Every numbered Theorem, Lemma and Corollary (all 451). Examples only when they are famous results or
// objects in their own right (28 of 194), under their printed number, with the most honest kind (Theorem, Definition,
// Model, Paradox, Phenomenon). Durrett never numbers definitions — they are boldface terms in running text — so the key
// ones are listed with the number of the preceding numbered item of the same section plus a letter (e.g. 2.2.3a = i.i.d.,
// defined just after Theorem 2.2.3), and `verify: true`. One unnumbered result is listed the same way: Pólya's urn
// (4.3.4a, the subsection "Polya's Urn Scheme" after Theorem 4.3.4).
//
// PER-CHAPTER COUNTS. Numbered results in the PDF (all listed) + examples listed (of the PDF's) + lettered items:
//   Ch 1   40 results  +  4 of 25 examples  + 12 definitions       Ch 6    8 results  + 7 of 18 examples + 2 definitions
//   Ch 2   66 results  +  7 of 32 examples  + 11 definitions       Ch 7   39 results  + 2 of 4 examples  + 2 definitions
//   Ch 3   70 results  +  3 of 45 examples  +  7 definitions       Ch 8   27 results  + 0 of 7 examples  + 1 definition
//   Ch 4   74 results  +  1 of 25 examples  +  9 definitions       Ch 9   51 results  + 1 of 3 examples  + 1 definition
//          (+ Pólya's urn, 4.3.4a)                                  App A  23 results  + 0 of 7 examples  + 2 definitions
//   Ch 5   53 results  +  3 of 28 examples  +  8 definitions       Total 451 results + 28 examples + 56 lettered = 535 items
//
// AMBIGUITIES AND DECISIONS.
// - Appendix A ("Measure Theory Details") is numbered A.s.n in the book, which the atlas's number format cannot hold; it
//   is chapter 10 here, A.s.n ↦ 10.s.n, and every appendix item carries refs: ['PTE A.s.n'] with the printed number.
//   collection.js should get chapterLabels: { 10: 'Appendix A' } so the site prints "Theorem (Appendix A 3.1)".
// - Several appendix items repeat main-text results (A.1.1 = 1.1.9, A.1.2 = 1.1.10, A.1.4 = 2.1.6, A.3.1 = 2.1.21), and
//   8.4.1 repeats 2.4.9 (Glivenko–Cantelli); all are listed under both numbers and linked by `see`.
// - `deps` point backwards in reading order. Durrett often states a theorem and then proves it through lemmas numbered
//   after it (2.4.1 ← 2.4.2–2.4.4, 3.4.2 ← 3.4.3–3.4.4, 3.4.17 ← 3.4.18–3.4.19, 7.1.3 ← 7.1.4, 8.1.4 ← 8.1.9–8.1.10, …);
//   those lemmas cannot be deps of their theorem without pointing forward, so each such lemma names its theorem in `see`.
// - A definition printed at the very start of a section, before its first numbered item, takes that first item's number
//   plus a letter (x.y.0a is not allowed): 1.1.1a, 1.2.1a/b, 1.4.1a, 1.6.1a, 2.1.1a, 2.2.1a, 2.5.1a, 2.6.1a, 2.7.1a,
//   3.2.1a, 3.3.1a, 3.5.1a, 3.9.1a, 4.1.1a, 4.2.1a, 4.5.1a, 4.6.1a, 4.7.1a, 5.1.1a, 5.3.1a, 5.5.1a, 5.8.1a, 6.1.1a,
//   7.1.1a, 7.3.1a, 10.4.1a. These precede the item they are numbered after, so that item may cite them.
// - Main-text results whose proofs are deferred to the appendix (1.1.4/1.1.9 on Carathéodory, 2.1.21 on A.3.1, 4.1.2 on
//   Radon–Nikodym, 3.3.18 and 9.2.5 on differentiation under the integral) cite the appendix through `see`, not `deps`.
// - Cross-links (`see`) go to rca (measure, integration, Fourier, Radon–Nikodym), pma, fa, stat, stoch and to named
//   results elsewhere in the atlas (info AEP, nt equidistribution/Benford/Erdős–Kac, comb ballot/matching/Ulam, pde heat
//   and Dirichlet problems, popgen Wright–Fisher, algo birthday/coupon collector, behav/phil St. Petersburg, …).
// - Numbered remarks (Remark 8.2.1, 8.3.1–8.3.3, with their own counter) are commentary and are omitted. Unnumbered
//   statements inside exercises (the corollary of Fubini in Exercise 1.7.1, Scheffé's theorem in Section 3.2) are
//   omitted. Theorems 3.4.14 and 4.3.13 are stated without proof (references to Gnedenko–Kolmogorov and Athreya–Ney);
//   their deps are the results needed to state them.
// - Names follow Durrett where he names a result; otherwise the classical name (8.1.1 is printed "Skorokhod's
//   representation theorem" but is the Skorokhod embedding; 6.3.1 is the Kesten–Spitzer–Whitman range theorem;
